// op-backends.dbvl — per-op backend provenance for the saltpetre op surface.
//
// 2026-11-22 (plan 2026-11-22-saltpetre-op-backend-ledger.md): the per-op
// retirement ledger. One row per op dispatched by `run_node` in
// lib/saltpetre/exec.bv. The row states WHICH BACKEND serves the op and, when
// that backend is a delegated one, the exact measurement that retires the
// delegation.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// --------------------
// Saltpetre is the compat layer: today every op is Briev code (scalar CPU).
// The destination is Briev-EMITTED codegen (`.abv` / `accel` nodes /
// `attr declared_kind:` macros), per op, chosen by measurement. Without this
// ledger "how much of the op surface is genuinely Briev" is unanswerable, and
// a delegation could sit in the tree forever with nobody noticing.
//
// THE DOCTRINE BOUNDARY (why library FFI is allowed at all)
// ---------------------------------------------------------
// abv-gpu-doctrine.md §1.3 and
// 2026-11-05-intel-xpu-openvino-harvest.md §1 guardrail 1 both forbid THE
// COMPILER from delegating: "FFI lanes exist for users; they are not the
// codegen path", and the vendor runtime "NEVER enters Briev's kernel path".
// Neither forbids a LIBRARY from having an FFI arm — lib/std/ai/openvino.bv
// exists under exactly that reading. The escape hatch is legitimate BECAUSE it
// is temporary and tracked. That is what this file makes true:
//
//   every delegated row MUST name its `graduation` (the A/B that retires it),
//   and a `briev` row may not secretly call FFI.
//
// BACKEND FORMS
// -------------
//   briev         — served by Briev code (scalar CPU now; Briev-emitted later)
//   ffi(<lib>)    — delegated to a vendor OP-LEVEL lib (cublas, cudnn, onednn)
//   model(<lib>)  — delegated to a MODEL-LEVEL runtime (openvino)
//
// A `briev` row carries `graduation: none;` while the op makes no performance
// claim. An `ffi(`/`model(` row with `graduation: none;` is a build failure
// (tests/saltpetre_op_ledger.rs).
//
// GRADUATION FORMS
// ----------------
//   ffi(/model( rows:  graduation: <op> <shape> @ <device> vs <vendor> — <acceptance>;
//   briev rows:        graduation: none;                                    (no claim)
//                      graduation: promote: <shape> <dtype> @ <device> vs <vendor> <dtype>
//                                  — ratio <= <N>, BAD=0, CANARY_BAD=0, compiled-on-vulkan;
//                                  evidence: benchmarks/results/<doc>.md;
//
// A delegated row must name a shape, a device, a competitor, and an acceptance
// criterion, because a number only counts with a same-geometry correctness gate
// (house benchmark rule). "faster later" is not a graduation.
//
// `promote:` (2026-11-22, plan 2026-11-22-saltpetre-gemm-graduation.md) is the
// other direction: the op already IS Briev code and the claim is that it now
// holds its own against the vendor on a measured shape. Promotion is what moves
// an op out of "compat layer" status, so it is a claim with evidence, not a
// mood — the `evidence:` doc is parsed by the gate, which recomputes the ratio
// from the p50s recorded there and refuses the row if the claim is bigger than
// what was measured. One gate token is deliberately spelled
// `compiled-on-vulkan`: a device-lane number whose kernel did not compile on the
// device is a CPU number wearing a device label.
//
// KEEPING THIS FILE HONEST
// ------------------------
// tests/saltpetre_op_ledger.rs is the gate. It cross-checks this file against
// the actual `when node.op_type == "..."` arms in exec.bv in BOTH directions,
// so neither an op nor a row can go missing silently. Adding an op to
// run_node without a row here fails the build; adding a row for an op the
// dispatcher does not have fails it too.

// --- elementwise / unary ---------------------------------------------------

op: Sign; backend: briev; impl: tensor_sign; graduation: none;
op: Relu; backend: briev; impl: tensor_relu; graduation: none;
op: Sqrt; backend: briev; impl: tensor_sqrt; graduation: none;
op: Tanh; backend: briev; impl: tensor_tanh; graduation: none;
op: Sigmoid; backend: briev; impl: tensor_sigmoid; graduation: none;
op: Identity; backend: briev; impl: tensor_identity; graduation: none;

// --- binary (all five share tensor_binary with an op code) ------------------

op: Add; backend: briev; impl: tensor_binary; graduation: none;
op: Sub; backend: briev; impl: tensor_binary; graduation: none;
op: Mul; backend: briev; impl: tensor_binary; graduation: none;
op: Div; backend: briev; impl: tensor_binary; graduation: none;
op: Pow; backend: briev; impl: tensor_binary; graduation: none;

// --- convolution (conv2d handles 2-D; conv1d the 1-D case) -----------------
//
// The first graduation candidate after Gemm: conv2d is the hot kernel in the
// kiosk's YUNet path and the one with a measured ceil_* ceiling counterpart.

op: Conv; backend: briev; impl: conv2d; graduation: none;

// --- matrix multiply (the FIRST graduation candidate) ----------------------
//
// `matmul!` and `matmul_f16!` already exist in lib/std/numeric.bv with
// `attr declared_kind: matmul`, and the .abv ceiling kernels
// (benchmarks/intel_ptl/ceiling/ceil_gemm_*.abv) already measure the target.
// Graduating Gemm therefore needs no new compiler machinery — only the A/B.

// 2026-11-22 (P3, first graduation): promoted on a same-geometry, correctness-
// gated comparison at 512x512x512 f32 on PTL's Xe3 X7 358H iGPU. Device lane
// 0.36 ms median (0.235-0.371 over 6 runs) vs OpenVINO 2026.3.1 f32 0.481 ms
// p50, measured ratio 0.74x; the bound is stated as 2.0x because the two numbers
// are not identical units (Briev reports kernel time, OpenVINO a whole
// compiled-model call), so the tighter 0.74x is not the claim this row makes.
// Evidence, including both caveats and what is NOT claimed:
// benchmarks/results/2026-11-22-saltpetre-gemm-graduation-baseline.md.
op: Gemm; backend: briev; impl: gemm; graduation: promote: 512^3 f32 @ PTL-Xe3-vulkan vs openvino f32 — ratio <= 2.0, BAD=0, CANARY_BAD=0, compiled-on-vulkan; evidence: benchmarks/results/2026-11-22-saltpetre-gemm-graduation-baseline.md;

// --- pooling ---------------------------------------------------------------

op: MaxPool; backend: briev; impl: maxpool; graduation: none;

// --- reductions ------------------------------------------------------------

op: ReduceMean; backend: briev; impl: tensor_reduce_mean; graduation: none;
op: GlobalAveragePool; backend: briev; impl: global_avg; graduation: none;

// --- quantization ----------------------------------------------------------

op: QuantizeLinear; backend: briev; impl: quantize_linear; graduation: none;
op: DequantizeLinear; backend: briev; impl: dequantize_linear; graduation: none;

// --- shape / layout --------------------------------------------------------

op: Pad; backend: briev; impl: tensor_pad; graduation: none;
op: Concat; backend: briev; impl: tensor_concat; graduation: none;
op: Split; backend: briev; impl: split_into; graduation: none;
op: Gather; backend: briev; impl: tensor_gather; graduation: none;
op: Slice; backend: briev; impl: tensor_slice; graduation: none;
op: Unsqueeze; backend: briev; impl: tensor_unsqueeze; graduation: none;
op: Squeeze; backend: briev; impl: tensor_squeeze; graduation: none;
op: Transpose; backend: briev; impl: transpose4; graduation: none;
op: Resize; backend: briev; impl: resize_nearest; graduation: none;
op: Reshape; backend: briev; impl: reshape; graduation: none;
op: Flatten; backend: briev; impl: flatten; graduation: none;

// --- normalization ---------------------------------------------------------

op: BatchNormalization; backend: briev; impl: batchnorm; graduation: none;
